{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-94908" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{3}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-94908" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,1,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-94908" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ead9c6cf66c48ca0ffe8dde9b6f7a28f21db0354bb25d99f03ee33b6185ac8ed"} {"question" "q-94908" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-94908" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2}"] ["math" "3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2}"] ["math" "3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{3}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}+{y}_{3}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-94908" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{3}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-140987" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-140987" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-9 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -8 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -4) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n4{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-140987" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ead9c6cf66c48ca0ffe8dde9b6f7a28f21db0354bb25d99f03ee33b6185ac8ed"} {"question" "q-140987" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-140987" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}"] ["math" "4{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2}"] ["math" "4{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}_{1}^{2}-4{y}_{2}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-140987" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-94898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-94898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -3)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-94898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ead9c6cf66c48ca0ffe8dde9b6f7a28f21db0354bb25d99f03ee33b6185ac8ed"} {"question" "q-94898" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-94898" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-3{y}_{2}^{2}"] ["math" "-{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}"] ["math" "3{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}"] ["math" "3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}_{1}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-94898" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-94446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+2{x}_{2}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-94446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-94446" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ead9c6cf66c48ca0ffe8dde9b6f7a28f21db0354bb25d99f03ee33b6185ac8ed"} {"question" "q-94446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-94446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2{y}_{1}^{2}-3{y}_{2}^{2}"] ["math" "-3{y}_{2}^{2}"] ["math" "2{y}_{1}^{2}"] ["math" "3{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2}"] ["math" "3{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-94446" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-94449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Using the method of orthogonal transformations, find the canonical form of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-94449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -3 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-9) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -8) & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(\\lambda -4)(\\lambda +2) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =4,\\,-1,\\,-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n4{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2{y}_{3}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-94449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ead9c6cf66c48ca0ffe8dde9b6f7a28f21db0354bb25d99f03ee33b6185ac8ed"} {"question" "q-94449" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-94449" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}-2{y}_{3}^{2}"] ["math" "2{y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}"] ["math" "4{y}_{1}^{2}-2{y}_{2}^{2}"] ["math" "4{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2{y}_{3}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}+2{y}_{3}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-94449" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{2}^{2}-2{y}_{3}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-140997" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-140997" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-7\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =6,\\,1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]-1[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-1I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-140997" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ead9c6cf66c48ca0ffe8dde9b6f7a28f21db0354bb25d99f03ee33b6185ac8ed"} {"question" "q-140997" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-140997" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligne