2a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-95071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -40 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =5,\\,-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-8)I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-(-8)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-8)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{13},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-95071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ead9c6cf66c48ca0ffe8dde9b6f7a28f21db0354bb25d99f03ee33b6185ac8ed"} {"question" "q-95071" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-95071" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$"] ["text" "$D=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-95071" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-95069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the quadratic form $Q(x)=-3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2},$ find its canonical (diagonal) form ${y}^{T}\\,Dy$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that $x=Py$ defines the corresponding change of variables." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-95069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nEvery quadratic form can be reduced to its canonical (diagonal) form using orthogonal transformations.\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-3-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +8 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-2,\\,-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ \n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{1}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-4)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]-(-4)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-4)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing ${v}_{2}$ by its norm $‖{v}_{2}‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ (writing the eigenvalues in descending order) and the orthogonal matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/state.json" "file_sha256" "5347c8dfb77d642652a9030e47ca43feb15acf1c8431d4fb734df5990d220acd"} {"question" "q-95069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14076285/activity-